<h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

<h3>Is pilot scale always required for aerosols?</h3>
<p>Yes. Pilot scale is essential to verify crimp dimensions and gassing speeds. Skipping this step can lead to massive failure rates (leakage) on a commercial line, which is dangerous and costly.</p>

<h3>Can I use the same valve from the lab in commercial production?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes, but the <i>gassing capability</i> of the valve must be verified. Some valves restrict gas flow, slowing down commercial lines significantly.</p>

<h3>What is the biggest risk in aerosol scale-up?</h3>
<p>Leakage due to improper crimping dimensions (crimp depth and diameter) is the most common and critical failure mode.</p>

<h3>Do I need to test every single can?</h3>
<p>In commercial production, 100% of cans must pass through a hot water bath (usually 55°C) to check for leakage and pressure resistance. This is a mandatory safety standard.</p>

<p>If you are developing an aerosol product and are uncertain about its scalability or safety, an early technical assessment can prevent dangerous failures. Structured support during R&amp;D and pilot stages significantly improves commercial success.</p>

